  "summary": "Opulent residential architecture of wealthy Persian merchants featuring interior courtyards and intricate decorative elements.",
  "description": "Characterized by inward-facing designs with central courtyards, multiple stories, and lavish interior decorations including stucco work, stained glass, and painted ceilings. Built by affluent silk and textile merchants to display wealth while providing comfortable family living spaces in the hot desert climate.",
  "culturalContext": "Kashan's merchant houses (Tabatabaei, Borujerdi, Ameri — 1800s carpet-trade fortunes) are Iran's desert domestic showpieces: sunken courtyards (howz) with reflecting pools, windcatcher-cooled summer and winter wings, badgir-topped rooflines, and mirror-work, fresco and carved stucco interiors where merchants displayed silk-road wealth within Islamic privacy. The houses' reception rooms (panj-dari, golestan) hosted trade negotiations; today they operate as boutique hotels — the grand Persian house as living heritage.",
